 ESAT-6/CFP-10 secretion system of <italic>Mycobacterium marinum</italic> modulates phagosome maturation.</article-title><source>Cell Microbiol</source><volume>8</volume><fpage>1417</fpage><lpage>1429</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16922861</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ppat.1000430-Gao1"><label>6</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Gao</surname><given-names>LY</given-names></name><name><surname>Guo</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>McLaughlin</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Morisaki</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Engel</surname><given-names>JN</given-names></name><name><surname>Brown</surname><given-names>EJ</given-names></name></person-group><year>2004</year><article-title>A mycobacterial virulence gene cluster extending RD1 is required for cytolysis, bacterial spreading and ESAT-6 secretion.</article-title><source>Mol Microbiol</source><volume>53</volume><fpage>1677</fpage><lpage>1693</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15341647</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ppat.1000430-Smith1"><label>7</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Smith</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Manoranjan</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Pan</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Bohsali</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Xu</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2008</year><article-title>Evidence for pore formation in host cell membranes by ESX-1 secreted ESAT-6 and its role in <italic>Mycobacterium marinum</italic> escape from vacuole.</article-title><source>Infect Immun</source><volume>76</volume><fpage>5478</fpage><lpage>5487</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">18852239</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ppat.1000430-Davis1"><label>8</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Davis</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Ramakrishnan</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name></person-group><year>2009</year><article-title>The role of the granuloma in expansion and dissemination of early tuberculous infection.</article-title><source>Cell</source><volume>136</volume><fpage>37</fpage><lpage>49</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">19135887</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ppat.1000430-Clemens1"><label>9</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Clemens</surname><given-names>DL</given-names></name><name><surname>Lee</surname><given-names>B-Y</given-names></name><name><surname>Horwitz</surname><given-names>MA</given-names></name></person-group><year>2002</year><article-title>The <italic>Mycobacterium tuberculosis</italic> phagosome in human macrophages is isolated from the host cell cytoplasm.</article-title><source>Infect Immun</source><volume>70</volume><fpage>5800</fpage><lpage>5807</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12228310</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ppat.1000430-VanderWel1"><label>10</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Van der Wel</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><name><surname>Hava</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Houben</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Fluitsma</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>van Zon</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2007</year><article-title><italic>M. tuberculosis</italic> and <italic>M. leprae</italic> translocate from the phagolysosome to the cytosol in myeloid cells.</article-title><source>Cell</source><volume>129</volume><fpage>1287</fpage><lpage>1298</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17604718</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ppat.1000430-Hsu1"><label>11</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Hsu</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Hingley-Wilson</surname><given-names>SM</given-names></name><name><surname>Chen</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Chen</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Dai</surname><given-names>AZ</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2003</year><article-title>The primary mechanism of attenuation of bacillus Calmette-Gu&#x000e9;rin is a loss of secreted lytic function required for invasion of lung interstitial tissue.</article-title><source>Proc Natl Acad Sci USA</source><volume>100</volume><fpage>12420</fpage><lpage>12425</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14557547</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ppat.1000430-Stamm2"><label>12</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Stamm</surname><given-names>LM</given-names></name><name><surname>Morisaki</surname><given-names>JH</given-names></name><name><surname>Gao</surname><given-names>L-Y</given-names></name><name><surname>Jeng</surname><given-names>RL</given-names></name><name><surname>McDonald</surname><given-names>KL</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2003</year><article-title><italic>Mycobacterium marinum</italic> escapes from phagosomes and is propelled by actin-based motility.</article-title><source>J Exp Med</source><volume>198</volume><fpage>1361</fpage><lpage>1368</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14597736</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ppat.1000430-Perrin1"><label>13</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Perrin</surname><given-names>AJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Jiang</surname><given-names>X</given-names></name><name><surname>Birmingham</surname><given-names>CL</given-names></name><name><surname>So</surname><given-names>NS</given-names></name><name><surname>Brumell</surname><given-names>JH</given-names></name></person-group><year>2004</year><article-title>Recognition of bacteria in the cytosol of mammalian cells by the ubiquitin system.</article-title><source>Curr Biol</source><volume>14</volume><fpage>806</fpage><lpage>811</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15120074</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ppat.1000430-Mizushima1"><label>14</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Mizushima</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name></person-group><year>2007</year><article-title>Autophagy: process and function.</article-title><source>Genes Dev</source><volume>21</volume><fpage>2861</fpage><lpage>2873</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">18006683</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ppat.1000430-Kim1"><label>15</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Kim</surname><given-names>PK</given-names></name><name><surname>Hailey</surname><given-names>DW</given-names></name><name><surname>Mullen</surname><given-names>RT</given-names></name><name><surname>Lippincott-Schwartz</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><year>2008</year><article-title>Ubiquitin signals autophagic degradation of cytosolic proteins and peroxisomes.</article-title><source>Proc Natl Acad Sci USA</source><volume>105</volume><fpage>20567</fpage><lpage>20574</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">19074260</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="ppat.1000430-Birmingham1"><label>16</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Birmingham</surname><given-names>CL</given-names></name><name><surname>Smith</surname><given-names>AC</given-names></name><name><surname>Bakowski</surname><given-names>MA</given-names></name><name><surname>Yoshimori</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Brumell</surname><given-names>JH</given-names></name></person-group><year>2006</year><article-title>Autophagy controls <italic>Salmonella</italic> infection